@node	0.4, 0.5, 0.3, Chapter 0
@section Data

This section covers tuples, lists, the language's sum type, pattern
matching, and exhaustiveness. The data abstractions of @ref{2.1} onward
stand on these forms, and the interpreted data of the runtime
(@code{Value} and its variants) is one sealed hierarchy of exactly the kind
built here.

@heading Products: data classes

A @code{data class} declares a product: its constructor parameters are the
parts, and the compiler generates content equality, hashing, a printable
form, and a @code{copy} function from them.

@example kotlin
public data class Frac(
    val num: Long,
    val den: Long,
)

Frac(1L, 2L)
// => Frac(num=1, den=2)

Frac(1L, 2L) == Frac(1L, 2L)
// => true
@end example

@noindent
The standard library also has @code{Pair} and @code{Triple}, and this book
uses @code{Pair} where a pair of coordinates or a lookup key is genuinely
anonymous. Where the parts have meaning, a data class with those names
replaces them; that is the lesson of @ref{2.1} read in Kotlin. Constructor
parameters can be pulled apart again by destructuring, which the book's
listings use for @code{Pair} rows and @code{Map} entries:

@example kotlin
val (num, den) = Frac(3L, 4L)
num
// => 3
@end example

A @code{@@JvmInline} @newterm{value class} wraps one value with no
allocation at runtime. It gives the book's tagged scalars their host form:
the runtime's exact integers and symbols are value classes over @code{Long}
and @code{String}.

@example kotlin
@@JvmInline
public value class Money(
    public val cents: Long,
)

Money(250L)
// => Money(cents=250)
@end example

@heading Sums: sealed hierarchies and exhaustive @code{when}

A @code{sealed interface} declares a sum: a closed set of variants, known
at compile time, all declared in the same package. The @code{when} that
dispatches on a sealed value as an expression must cover every variant, and
writing no @code{else} is what buys that guarantee from the compiler:

@example kotlin
public sealed interface Shape

public data class Circle(
    val r: Double,
) : Shape

public data class Rect(
    val w: Double,
    val h: Double,
) : Shape

public fun area(s: Shape): Double =
    when (s) @{
        is Circle -> PI * s.r * s.r
        is Rect -> s.w * s.h
    @}

area(Circle(1.0))
// => 3.141592653589793

area(Rect(2.0, 3.0))
// => 6.0
@end example

@noindent
The @code{is} test both discriminates the variant and binds its parts,
which is this book's pattern matching. The guarantee is the point, and
exercise 0.3 turns it into a compile experiment: add a variant, and every
@code{when} over the type stops compiling until its case is written. Data
directed dispatch, where the set of types must stay open at runtime, is a
different mechanism for a different job; @ref{2.4} builds it as a registry
for exactly that reason.

@heading Lists and persistence

A @code{List<T>} is the default sequence: random access, content equality,
and no mutation operations. Where the book needs a value whose versions
persist, the lists are @code{PersistentList<T>} from
@file{kotlinx.collections.immutable}: every operation returns a new list
that shares its structure with the old one, and the old one stays intact.

@example kotlin
val xs = persistentListOf(1L, 2L)
val ys = xs.adding(3L)

xs
// => [1, 2]

ys
// => [1, 2, 3]
@end example

@noindent
This is the everyday form for the environments of @ref{3.2} and @ref{4.1}
and the tables of @ref{3.3.3}, which are persistent maps on the same
principle. The interpreted lists of the runtime are a different beast
again: the cons cells of @ref{2.2.1}, whose pairs are mutable in chapter 3
(@ref{0.5}).

@heading @code{==} and @code{===}

Kotlin separates the two equalities the book needs. @code{==} is content
equality: for a data class it compares part by part. @code{===} is
identity: it asks whether two names hold the very same object.

@example kotlin
Frac(1L, 2L) == Frac(1L, 2L)
// => true

Frac(1L, 2L) === Frac(1L, 2L)
// => false
@end example

@noindent
The original book distinguishes two equalities: structural equality maps
to @code{==} on the data types, and pointer identity maps to @code{===}
where identity is the question. Chapter 3
makes identity observable on purpose: the runtime's mutable pairs compare
by identity, so identity comparison on pairs and @code{===} agree by
construction (@ref{0.5}).

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.3}Exercise 0.3:} Model a small binary tree as a
sealed hierarchy with a @code{Leaf} holding a value and a @code{Node}
holding two subtrees. Write an exhaustive @code{when} that computes the
depth of a tree in levels: a leaf has depth 1, and a node has one more than
its deeper child. Then add a third variant of your choosing to the
hierarchy and rebuild: the walker must fail to compile with an
exhaustiveness error naming the missing variant. Record the compiler's
message, restore the two-variant tree, and say in one sentence what the
compiler bought you.
